  "mathematical_change": "F in C0 suffices for bounded uniqueness under connected and dense positivity; all retained proof mathematics is unchanged.",
  "analytic_review_groups": [
    "The connected compact Hermitian setting distinguishes scalar uniqueness from potential uniqueness; every Sobolev or coefficient product retains its explicitly stated regularity.",
    "The Lipschitz gradient maps the exceptional null set to a null set; perturbed contact points have second-order Taylor expansions and converge to a point of the continuous positive-density threshold set.",
    "Strict weak comparison and the annular exponential barrier prove the measurable strong maximum principle without differentiating its coefficients.",
    "The finite mixed cofactor is bounded above and below by fixed multiples of (A+B)^(n-1); the local coefficient upper bound is necessary for the converse ellipticity implication, while n=1 has T=1.",
    "The maximum and minimum occur in positive-density components; continuity extends their values to closures. Connectedness and density justify the two-component criterion, including a connected hypersurface without a chosen coorientation.",
    "Parallel transverse segments and quadratic growth of a nonnegative C1,1 density force the square-root lower volume bound at a separator. Dominated convergence gives the inverse-square-root sufficient condition.",
    "The weighted cofactor identity uses only the C2 approximation of w^2 and distributional ddc-closedness; it annihilates all reachable directions without claiming a symmetric divergence operator.",
    "The real principal symbol has factor 1/(8*pi) and the skew off-diagonal blocks prescribed by complex differentiation; it is semipositive and has the same range as the cofactor quadratic form.",
    "The shrunken tangent ball and boundary lens prove the vanishing normal quadratic form. Nearest-point upper derivatives and Gronwall propagate extrema under locally Lipschitz flows in both time directions.",
    "Pushforwards under finite smooth flows contain nested brackets; full rank gives open orbits by the inverse function theorem, without a constant-rank assumption or a smooth matrix square root.",
    "Unweighted integration has precisely one half of the w^2 ddcT pairing. On a surface ddcT=2*ddc omega; in dimension three the additional potential-dependent term survives the Gauduchon condition.",
    "Simultaneous congruence diagonalization and product-one AM-GM give strict quadratic signs in dimensions two and three; equality at positive determinant forces A=B.",
    "Strong W2,n approximation yields all required Ln products in L1. The threefold torsion identity has the minus sign, while the assumed sign of ddc omega reverses the integral inequality. Almost-everywhere equality upgrades to qpsh representatives.",
    "The Hopf threefold ddc omega=-alpha^2 supplies the sign condition despite its infinite upper mass; this does not impose bounded mass on the general uniqueness results.",
    "The bounded connected-positive uniqueness theorem requires only F in C0: compactness bounds F, the earlier Lp continuity theorem makes bounded solutions continuous, and none of the retained proof differentiates F.",
    "Weak extremum localization fixes the reserve t*delta, then the comparison height. The mixed determinant lower bounds yield positive common mass in the near-extremum set where F exceeds kappa*G.",
    "Closed local Bedford\u2013Taylor expansions and uniform continuous weights construct the positive energy measure independently of regularization and make ddcT a finite signed measure.",
    "Zero total mass and first moment of the pushforward of ddcT imply a compactly supported Lipschitz density k for the energy pushforward. Its nonnegativity and absolute continuity exclude mass on every level set.",
    "The constant determinant lower bound passes to the absolutely continuous coefficients through convolution. Fatou, the cofactor bound det/trace and the locally finite trace give the L1 coercivity estimate.",
    "The integrals of 1/(k+delta) diverge at the extremal level; normalized cutoff energies tend to zero. The limiting indicator has zero distributional gradient on the connected dense positive set, proving zero common mass of extrema.",
    "Only the independently selected smooth auxiliary density g enters the classical local Dirichlet problem. A strict quadratic subsolution meets its hypotheses; the real contact-set determinant bound has factor 4^n and gives a uniform L2-based depth bound.",
    "Fixed finite chart margins precede the common small scale tau; convex smoothing, zero extension and averaging yield a globally strict test potential with a determinant lower bound independent of density height and derivatives.",
    "The test constant epsilon is chosen before A. The compact zero-volume set K intersect {F>=epsilon/2} admits a small-volume smooth cutoff; the L2 norm stays fixed while G>F near all of K. Continuity gives the contradictory thin extremal neighborhood.",
    "For a bounded measurable density with positive essential lower bound, coercivity holds on every chart and the same argument dominates the essential upper bound near the zero-volume extremal set.",
    "Ordinary comparison is equivalent to invariant bounded mass via the maximum and ordered contact; conversely constant translates compare whole masses and recover the Guan\u2013Li conditions, with n=1 handled by degrees.",
    "The doubled indicator density on one strict sublevel has the correct total mass. Its auxiliary continuous solution and the strict mixed inequality force zero sublevel mass; bounded domination proves uniqueness without dividing by the density.",
    "On projective three-space the three rank-one directions are independent; their cube is 6xyz. Exact cancellations on the open band, smooth joining at its endpoints and projective extensions produce two forms with the same measure.",
    "A common exact correction restores a strict Hermitian background without changing the solution forms. The nonzero differential is checked at r=0; bounded mass follows from the closed Fubini\u2013Study comparison, not from mass invariance of the new background.",
    "The radial balance has a continuum of switching pairs; its Lipschitz slopes stay admissible and their continuous first derivatives add no hypersurface measure. Symmetric data give the block-exchange solution, with selection left to the referenced later theorem.",
    "For the thin zero set, left backward and right forward barriers trap the scalar ODE. The normalized slope and all its derivatives grow at most polynomially, so multiplication by the flat bump gives smooth infinite-order endpoint extension.",
    "Quadratic density zeros still trap the ODE; its logarithmic factor has the signed inverse-square-distance bounds giving exp(-A/d) decay. Polynomial bounds for every differentiated rational coefficient prove all-order flat extension.",
    "For a real analytic density, positive divided differences remove endpoint division, the two triples have exactly the prescribed equal product, and determinant-preserving patching extends over the projective ends. The exact correction is strict and nonclosed; product dimensions preserve rank n-1, and transverse tubular charts give the sharp square-root volume and alpha<1/2 integrability threshold."
  ],
